The '''2022 Walsall Metropolitan Borough Council election''' took place on 5 May 2022 to elect members of [[Walsall Council]]. This was on the same day as [[2022 United Kingdom local elections|other local elections]]. 21 of the 60 seats were up for election, with 1 ward (Willenhall South) electing 2 councillors.<ref>{{cite web |title=Election timetable in England |url=https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/election-timetable-in-england/election-timetable-in-england#metropolitan-districts |website=GOV.UK |access-date=9 April 2022}}</ref>

==Background==
Since its creation in 1974, Walsall has varied between the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservatives]] and [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]]. Between 1973 and [[2004 Walsall Metropolitan Borough Council election|2004]], Walsall had been under Labour control from 1973 to 1976, 1980 to 1982, 1988 to 1992, 1995 to 1996, and [[1999 Walsall Metropolitan Borough Council election|1999]] to [[2000 Walsall Metropolitan Borough Council election|2000]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Walsall Metropolitan Borough Council Election Results 1973-2012 |url=http://www.electionscentre.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2015/06/Walsall-1973-2012.pdf |website=Elections Centre |access-date=9 April 2022}}</ref> It was then under Conservative control between 2004 and [[2011 Walsall Metropolitan Borough Council election|2011]], before reverting to [[no overall control]] and being retaken by the Conservatives in 2019.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Coussins |first1=Jordan |title=Walsall Council local elections 2019: Conservatives take control |url=https://www.birminghammail.co.uk/black-country/walsall-council-local-elections-2019-16186824 |access-date=9 April 2022 |work=BirminghamLive |date=3 May 2019}}</ref> In the [[2021 Walsall Metropolitan Borough Council election|2021 elections]], the Conservatives gained 5 seats with 54.1% of the vote, Labour lost 3 with 36.3%, and the [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] lost their 2 seats on the council with 2.5%.

The seats up for election this year were last elected in [[2018 Walsall Metropolitan Borough Council election|2018]]. In that election, the Conservatives gained 5 seats, Labour lost 2, and [[UK Independence Party|UKIP]] lost their 3 seats on the council.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Leather |first1=Harry |title=Walsall local election results: Tory gains leave them in pole position |url=https://www.expressandstar.com/news/politics/2018/05/04/walsall-local-election-results-tory-gains-leave-them-in-pole-position/ |access-date=9 April 2022 |work=Express and Star}}</ref>

